{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Why do you cry out to Me</b> — Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] (on Exodus 14:15) said that this refers to Moses standing in place of all Israel, for they were crying out to him, as it says, \"And the children of Israel cried out to YHWH.\" But if so, why does it say <b>why do you cry out to Me</b>? It would be fitting for them to cry out. Perhaps he [Ibn Ezra] means: why do you let them cry? Speak to them and let them journey onward, for I have already told you \"and I will be glorified through Pharaoh\" (Exodus 14:4). But our Rabbis said (Mekhilta, here) that Moses himself was crying out and praying, and this is the correct interpretation, for he did not know what to do. Even though HaShem had told him \"and I will be glorified through Pharaoh,\" he did not know how to conduct himself, for they were at the edge of the sea and the enemy was pursuing and closing in, and he was praying that YHWH would instruct him in the way He would choose. This is the meaning of <b>why do you cry out to Me</b> — you should have asked what to do, and you have no need to cry out, for I have already made known to you \"and I will be glorified through Pharaoh.\" And Scripture did not record Moses' cry, because it is included within Israel's."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:07:48.265550513Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}